## Verdict: FAIL — on `doc_sync` only

The engineering is genuinely good and I verified it rather than trusting the diff:

• **Fix is at the emission point.** `HINT_GLYPH` is one statement prepended only on the non-plain path, so a seventh rule can't reintroduce a glyph on the plain path. The wrapper reads `is_plain_output()` — not the core — preserving the Day-177 "wrapper is the only global read" invariant the split was made to establish.
• **Touchpoints complete.** `contextual_hint_with` gained a third parameter; all four call sites (`:963`, `:2653`, `:2746`, `:2754`) are updated in this diff. I specifically checked the source-level guard at :2406, which was the plausible silent breakage — it keys on the wrapper's signature, which is unchanged, so it still resolves.
• **The guard pins the property, not the trace** (the Day-194 requirement). I ran it: it passes, asserts both directions with whole-string `assert_eq!` across all six rows, and makes the distinct-category count itself an assertion — so a row duplicating its neighbour would leave a hint string untested and still fail.
• **Register is honest.** wc -l = 2788, matching the pasted entry exactly; gate passes 28/28 with zero warnings.

Why it still fails:

git diff --name-only HEAD~2 returns three files and CLAUDE.md is not among them, while the task named it in Files: and gave it a dedicated ## Docs section. This matters concretely rather than procedurally: the diff changes user-visible output, and CLAUDE.md still carries contextual_hint_with(&mut HashSet<&'static str>, ctx) — the two-parameter signature — plus the claims "Production behaviour is byte-identical" and "every plain parameter and glyph-free path is unaffected." Those describe the exact function this diff changed, in the file re-injected as authoritative context every session. That is the src/gasp.rs staleness class the repo records as having cost eight sessions, and the evaluator rejected two prior tasks (#864's payment, the Day-194 commands_spawn.rs task) for this identical omission.

Two things I noticed while checking and recorded rather than treating as separate failures: the round's own result line reports that CLAUDE.md's rtk.rs bullet ("the only stderr notice in the codebase that consulted neither") is now false — a correction the round discovered and didn't land — and the round-96 experiment_result has no `positive_control` field, where round 95 does, so the mandated mutate→run→restore with its red width and sorted green is unrecorded.

The remedy is small and the work beside it is sound: the fix, guard, ledger and register should survive; what's missing is the CLAUDE.md write-up and the positive-control record.
